course: biology
lesson: 307
title: Beetles of every kind: stag beetles, dung beetles, fireflies, bombardiers and weevils
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: With hundreds of thousands of kinds, beetles have tried almost every way of living. Some wrestle with antlers, some roll dung, some glow, some fire hot spray and some drill into nuts with a long snout.
explain: Picture a dung beetle on an African night: head down, pushing a ball of dung bigger than itself backwards with its back legs. It climbs on top, turns round to check, and rolls on in a straight line, steering by the faint light of the Milky Way.
explain: On a summer evening a field can twinkle with fireflies, which are beetles making cold light in their tails, each kind with its own flash code. In a few places thousands flash in time together.
explain: Big jaws and horns are mostly for wrestling rivals, and many of these giants spend years as grubs in rotting wood, so an old log in a garden is a beetle nursery.
fact: The male stag beetle's huge jaws look like a deer's antlers, and males use them to wrestle, lifting and flipping rivals; the grubs live in rotting wood for up to about six years, the adults only a few weeks.
fact: Rolling dung beetles push the ball backwards with their back legs, head down, rolling it away so others cannot steal it, then bury it as food and a nursery; they can roll a ball many times their own weight.
fact: Scientists in South Africa found some dung beetles roll in a straight line using the light of the Milky Way; with tiny caps over their eyes they got lost, and they climb on the ball to turn and check their direction.
fact: Australia's own dung beetles were used to dry kangaroo pellets, not big wet cow pats, so cow pats piled up and fed flies; from the 1960s scientists brought in dung beetles from Africa and Europe to bury the dung, feed the soil and cut fly numbers.
fact: Fireflies are beetles that make light in their tails with a chemical reaction; the light is almost cold, and each kind has its own flash pattern to find a partner.
fact: In the Great Smoky Mountains of the USA and parts of Southeast Asia, crowds of fireflies flash in time, each adjusting to its neighbours; in Britain the glow-worm is a beetle too, and its wingless female glows in the grass.
fact: The bombardier beetle mixes two chemicals in a chamber in its tail, which heat up and pop out as a spray near boiling point, fired in very fast pulses, hundreds a second, and aimed at ants, spiders or frogs.
fact: In a Japanese study, bombardier beetles swallowed by toads sprayed inside the toads' stomachs, the toads threw them up, and most beetles walked away unharmed, a very rare escape in nature.
fact: Weevils are beetles with a long snout with tiny jaws at the tip, used to drill holes in nuts and seeds for their eggs; they are one of the biggest beetle families, with tens of thousands of kinds.
fact: Rice and grain weevils lay eggs inside grains, where the grubs grow, so grain is kept cool and dry; in Alabama the boll weevil ate so much cotton that farmers grew other crops, and the town of Enterprise built a statue to it.
fact: The male Hercules beetle has two long horns, one from the head and one from the body above it, used like pincers to lift and throw rivals; with its horns it can be about 17 centimetres long.
exercise: What does a male stag beetle use its antler-like jaws for? | Wrestling and flipping rival males | Digging deep tunnels for its grubs in the soil | Cutting leaves to eat | Catching flies in the air
exercise: How long can a stag beetle grub live in rotting wood? | Up to about six years | For just about six days | About sixty years or more | It never lives in wood
exercise: How does a rolling dung beetle push its ball? | Backwards with its back legs, head down | Forwards with its jaws, like a bulldozer | By kicking it hard with its front legs | By rolling on top like a circus act
exercise: Why does a dung beetle roll its ball away from the pile? | So others cannot steal it before it is buried | So it can play football with the other beetles | So the dung dries into a stone | So it can find water
exercise: How did scientists show dung beetles steer by the Milky Way? | Beetles with tiny caps over their eyes got lost | Beetles flew up towards the stars and came back | Beetles rolled their balls straight only on cloudy nights | Beetles drew maps in the sand
exercise: Why did Australia bring in dung beetles from abroad? | Its own beetles handled kangaroo pellets, not big wet cow pats | Its own dung beetles had all been eaten up by hungry kangaroos | To make cow pats bigger | To win a beetle race
exercise: What good did the new dung beetles do in Australia? | They buried dung, fed the soil and cut fly numbers | They ate all the kangaroos' food | They built little new roads across the farms | They scared away the cows
exercise: How does a firefly make light? | A chemical reaction in its tail | A tiny battery hidden in its body | Sunlight stored up during the long summer day | Rubbing its wings together very fast
exercise: Why does each kind of firefly have its own flash pattern? | To find a partner of the same kind | To scare owls away | To light the way home through the dark woods | To tell the time
exercise: What happens in the Great Smoky Mountains on some summer nights? | Crowds of fireflies flash in time together | All the fireflies turn green at the very same moment | Fireflies fly to the Moon | Fireflies sing in a choir
exercise: In Britain, which glow-worm glows in the grass? | The wingless female, which is a beetle | The male, which is a worm | The grub, which is a kind of caterpillar | None, glow-worms only live in caves
exercise: How hot is a bombardier beetle's spray? | Near boiling point | As cold as ice from a freezer | Warm like bath water | It is not hot at all
exercise: What happened to bombardier beetles swallowed by toads in a Japanese study? | They sprayed, the toads threw them up and most walked away | They were all digested at once, like any other beetle | They lived inside the toads for years | They turned into toads
exercise: What is a weevil's long snout for? | Drilling holes in nuts and seeds to lay eggs | Smelling flowers from far away across the fields | Sucking blood like a mosquito | Blowing bubbles underwater
exercise: Why are grain stores kept cool and dry? | To stop weevils that lay eggs inside grains | To make the grain grow faster and bigger in the store | To keep the mice warm | Because weevils love the cold
exercise: Why did the town of Enterprise in Alabama build a statue to the boll weevil? | It ate so much cotton that farmers grew other crops | It saved the town from a great flood by eating the dam | It was the town's favourite pet | It was found inside a famous statue
exercise: What does a male Hercules beetle do with its horns? | Lifts and throws rival males | Digs in the soil for buried dung | Holds its eggs safe until they hatch | Stabs ripe fruit to drink the juice
test: Count them: a male Hercules beetle has 2 horns. A box at the museum holds 5 males. How many horns? | 10 | 5 | 7 | 15
test: Count them: 4 dung beetles each roll a ball away from a pile, but a thief steals 1 ball. How many balls get buried? | 3 | 4 | 5 | 1
test: Count them: 3 stag beetles and 2 dung beetles meet on a log. How many wing cases altogether? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: In The Princess and the Frog, Ray the firefly glows like a hot little lamp. What is true about real firefly light? | It is almost cold, with nearly no heat wasted | It is hotter than a light bulb | It comes from a tiny fire burning in the tail | It is moonlight the firefly stores up in its body
test: «there's a beetle that farts boiling acid lol» What is closer to the truth? | The bombardier beetle mixes two chemicals that heat up and spray out | It breathes real fire from its mouth, like a little dragon | It is a myth, no beetle sprays anything | It sprays cold water to wash itself
test: What would happen if the Milky Way was hidden by thick cloud all night? | Dung beetles that steer by it might wander instead of rolling straight | Dung beetles would fly up through the clouds to find it | Dung beetles would roll faster | Nothing, beetles cannot see light
test: «just chuck the old log in the bin, it's rotting» What would a beetle fan say? | Leave it, stag beetle grubs may be living in it for years | Burn it, rotten wood is useless to every living thing | Rotten logs only hold snakes | Beetles hate old wood
test: A frog snaps up a bombardier beetle. What would most likely happen next? | The beetle sprays and the frog lets go or spits it out | The frog enjoys a nice hot meal and hops off | The beetle freezes the frog | Nothing, frogs never eat beetles of any kind
test: You open an old bag of rice and see tiny beetles. What most likely happened? | Grain weevils laid eggs inside the grains and the grubs grew | The rice turned into beetles | The beetles came from the shop's ceiling that day | Rain made beetles grow in the bag
test: Why would a town thank a pest that ate its cotton? | The farmers switched to other crops and did better | The weevils were tasty to eat | The weevils made the cotton stronger and softer than before | The weevils built the town hall
test: A firefly flashes a different pattern from the ones around it. What is likely? | It is a different kind of firefly | It is broken and needs new batteries | It is the leader of all the fireflies | It is trying to cool its tail down
exam: Identify the means by which certain dung beetles of South Africa maintain a straight course while rolling dung at night. | Orientation by the light of the Milky Way | Orientation by the strong scent of the dung pile | Orientation by the sound of nearby running rivers | Orientation by the warmth of the sandy ground
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the light produced by fireflies? | It is produced by a chemical reaction and is accompanied by almost no heat | It is produced by combustion within the abdomen and is accompanied by intense heat | It is reflected moonlight stored within the abdomen | It is produced by friction between the wings
exam: Ascertain the reason for which dung beetles were introduced into Australia from the nineteen sixties onwards. | The native beetles were suited to kangaroo pellets and did not dispose of cattle dung | The native beetles had consumed all available vegetation | The cattle of Australia produced no dung whatsoever | The introduced beetles were intended to control kangaroos
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the defence of the bombardier beetle. | Two chemicals are mixed in the abdomen and discharged as a hot spray in rapid pulses | A venom is injected through the jaws into the attacker | The beetle conceals itself by changing colour | The beetle releases a cold, sweet liquid that attracts its attackers and then entangles them
